candidates.demonstrated_gap:
Emdash is an operator cockpit over native CLIs, not a new worker runtime. What it offers:
- a Git worktree and branch per agent;
- detection of installed provider CLIs (Claude Code, Codex and others), so native accounts are kept;
- issue intake from GitHub, Linear, Jira, GitLab and others;
- diff review, PR creation, CI checks and merge from one place;
- remote projects over SSH/SFTP, with credentials kept in the OS keychain;
- local-first operation: state in a local SQLite database, no code or chats sent to Emdash servers, telemetry optional (TELEMETRY_ENABLED=false);
- Linux x64 AppImage/DEB/RPM and macOS arm64 builds, which fit both hosts.

Costs:
- It writes marker-tagged lifecycle-hook entries into each agent's user-level config, which collides with this stack's user-scope hook carriers.
- Removing a workspace kills its sessions, runs a non-fatal, time-boxed teardown, then removes the worktree (ADR 0003, superseded by ADR 0005).
- The material read does not document whether a failed attempt's dirty worktree is kept.

None of the five open gaps is about a review screen: head-to-head comparison, a Worktrunk model-child run, crash/reboot, accounting and the restricted role. So there is no targeted trial now.

Maintenance: 2143 default-branch commits since 2026-07-08; latest 7ae750f2c11b on 2026-10-06.

candidates.comparison_that_would_overturn:
On a scratch repository, run the same frozen batch of 3 parallel tasks, one forced to fail, two ways:
- Emdash v1.2.7 driving Claude Code and Codex in its worktrees;
- native Claude Code subagents with isolation: worktree, plus Worktrunk.

Measure:
- writes outside each owned worktree (must be zero);
- whether the failed attempt's diff and logs can still be recovered after the task is closed;
- whether merges happen only after an explicit review;
- operator time to review all three;
- complete provider usage;
- a before/after diff of the ~/.claude and ~/.codex config, to show any interference with the hook carriers.

Emdash earns a targeted trial only if it keeps failed results that the native path loses, or cuts review time without hook conflicts.

On the macOS arm64 host, Superset's supported target, run the Superset MCP server driven by a Claude Code coordinator. Compare it with native Claude Code subagents (isolation: worktree) plus Worktrunk. Use the same frozen batch of 3 tasks, with one forced failure and one kill of the app or lead process mid-run.

Measure:
- no writes outside the owned worktrees;
- the failed attempt's worktree and transcript are kept;
- whether sessions survive the kill (Superset's daemon compared with native /resume);
- merges happen only after review;
- complete usage;
- whether a Superset account or the paid tier was required.

A targeted trial becomes justified only if local use is confirmed to need no account, and the restart survival holds where the native path loses in-process teammates.

candidates.demonstrated_gap:
Decisive reason: the vendor has sunset the project. The README carries the banner 'Vibe Kanban is sunsetting', added in commit 9f1015036c96 on 2026-04-24, the same day as the last release.

The announcement says:
- bloop, the company, is shutting down;
- the project 'will live on, open source and community maintained';
- remote services (kanban issues, comments, projects, organisations) end after 30 days, and the app moves to a fully local architecture;
- a community roadmap will follow 'over the next few weeks'.

It is not stale under the 90-day rule: 10 default-branch commits since 2026-07-08, the latest d5cbb5380fa0 on 2026-09-19. But it has had no release for more than five months, and no community maintainer is named in the README or announcement read.

Its features are matched by actively released members of the class (emdash, superset): a workspace per task with its own branch, diff comments sent to agents, PR creation and merge, and support for 10+ CLIs. It closes no gap of its own.
